summary:
Astro-Twin is a web-based health-triage tool that turns astronaut health data into an interactive "digital twin." A body map colors each region green, yellow or red according to its readings:

the head for brain and vision;
the torso for heart and blood;
the limbs for bone and muscle;
a surrounding field for radiation and immune health.
Crews can see what needs attention without reading a lab report. The tool has three modes:

a mission scenario with ISS, Moon and Mars settings, where limits change with gravity and the communication delay to Earth;
playback of real Inspiration4 crew bloodwork from NASA's Open Science Data Repository, graded against each lab's own normal range;
a Cognitive Lab of short reaction, processing-speed and memory games, which fills the brain readings that bloodwork can't provide.
This matters because missions are moving farther from Earth. From Mars, a message can take up to about 22 minutes each way, so astronauts will need to understand their own health data without waiting for experts on the ground. Astro-Twin makes complex health data quick to read, and it labels clearly which data is real and which is simulated.
